[ceiling] Unavoidable square capture interfaces: these Z169.45 flats are the rear rail catches above mating feet at Z168.70. They retain 0.25 mm running air plus the two supported-face allowances. The square overlap resists lifting without a ramp. Tree(auto)/Default support can leave through the empty slide channels; physical cleanup and loaded behavior remain unqualified.
alternatives: Sloping introduces a camming bearing. Filling blocks the mating foot; removing overlap loses capture.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=100.875 y=339.925 z=169.450
click: x=-100.875 y=393.050 z=169.450
click: x=-100.875 y=270.675 z=169.450

[ceiling] Unavoidable lifting interfaces: these Z29.25 flats retain the finger bearings, R6 end returns and 12 mm handhold roofs. Support can leave through the downward/outward openings and empty seam before assembly. Actual cleanup, bare-surface comfort and loaded lifting remain unqualified.
alternatives: Sloping changes the finger bearing and opening. Filling beneath it blocks upward entry; retain the named lifting profile.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=-97.979 y=224.075 z=29.250
click: x=97.979 y=224.075 z=29.250

[ceiling] Unavoidable head bearings: these Z40.8 flats seat the two lower M5 heads at the top of their Ø9 openings, which run up from the handhold ceiling and end on the plane of the handhold inner wall's finger face. Support leaves down through the opening and handhold before hardware placement. Geometric access does not qualify cleanup.
alternatives: Sloping tilts the head seat. Moving the floor changes the screw's reach into its insert; rounding the opening's square inboard side leaves a knife edge where its circle touches the wall face.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=-98.250 y=208.900 z=40.800
click: x=98.250 y=208.900 z=40.800

[thin] Unavoidable at the matched R6 roll: the lower Y-stack head opening (Ø9) leaves a 2.46 mm outboard rim where the handhold's R6 lifting-edge roll and its additive print chamfer meet the ceiling plane; the rim reaches 3 mm about 1.2 mm above it. The opening's axis is fixed by the head passing the handhold's 3 mm inner wall, which stands 0.25 mm off the cold core. A 3 mm rim at the plane needs that roll at R4.5, against Derek's 2026-09-19 request that the bottom grips match the top's rounding "all aspects of it, for all rounded edges".
alternatives: An R4.5 handhold lifting-edge roll gives a 3.03 mm rim; a local boss round each recess breaks the matched roll at the fastener.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=105.247 y=209.512 z=29.327
click: x=-105.247 y=209.512 z=29.327

[slope] The linter is wrong for this channel: this 45° face is the roof of the relief over the lower Y-stack screw passage where it crosses back-bottom's upper knuckle underside (`_y_bore_reliefs`). It rises toward the passage inside a slot of the passage's own width, so both of its rising edges stand on the slot's walls 5.6 mm apart and each layer of it is carried at both ends as well as by the layer below; the rule reads one wall and its rising edge, a slope hanging sideways off a single wall.
alternatives: A level roof over the slot is a down-facing ceiling. A roof rising off the slot walls, a gable, dives under the 45° knuckle face along a V and leaves a ridge down the slot. A vertical end wall where the roof lands meets the knuckle face on a 45° arris. The rule could count a slope whose two rising edges stand on opposed walls as carried.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=95.719 y=208.900 z=140.148

[thin] The linter is wrong here: each segment runs from the back rail's under-flare, at its end face on Y456.3, across the open notch between that face and the rear corner column's R15 arc to the column's top edge; its midpoint stands outside both, in air. The flare ends with the arm, so no 45° tip meets the mouth past the arm's end.
alternatives: Running the flare on into the column fills the notch but carries its 45° tip to the mouth with no arm above it, a knife edge; filling the notch through the column's height leaves a 37° wedge where the arc meets the end plane. The rule could require material along the segment before classifying a thickness.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
click: x=-94.362 y=456.203 z=159.388
click: x=94.362 y=456.203 z=159.388

[thin] Specific required legend relief: each reading is a stroke of the raised disposal warning (hardware/markings/README.md: UL 60335-2-24 7.1DV.4.1(d), letters at least 6.4 mm), 6.5 mm Helvetica Bold caps standing 0.6 mm proud of back-bottom's +Y wall, or one of its full stops. At that size a bold stroke is 0.96-1.41 mm across; the readings run 0.93-1.43 mm, across a stroke at its face or from a stroke's level top to its 45° underside.
alternatives: 3 mm strokes would need letters about 15 mm tall, and a lighter weight thins every stroke further.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-back-bottom.step
